# Modern JavaScript Standards

When writing or editing JavaScript, use current ECMAScript style. Prefer modern syntax over legacy patterns.

## Declarations & scope

- Use `const` by default; `let` only when reassignment is required
- Never use `var`
- Prefer ES modules (`import` / `export`) over IIFEs or globals
- Prefer `for...of` (with `.entries()` when an index is needed) over `forEach` with `function`

## Syntax

- Use arrow functions for callbacks and short handlers
- Use template literals instead of string concatenation with `+`
- Destructure objects and arrays when reading multiple fields
- Prefer optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) over manual null checks

## DOM (when applicable)

- Prefer `document.querySelector` / `querySelectorAll` over `getElementById` / `getElementsBy*`
- Prefer `element.append()` / `prepend()` over `appendChild` when adding nodes
- Prefer `textContent` + `createElement` over `innerHTML` for dynamic text
- Use `DocumentFragment` when appending many siblings in one pass

## Examples

```javascript
// ❌ BAD
var name = site.name;
list.appendChild(li);
btn.innerHTML = "<strong>" + site.name + "</strong>";
sites.forEach(function (site, i) {
  /* ... */
});

// ✅ GOOD
const { name, country } = site;
list?.append(fragment);
label.textContent = name;
for (const [i, site] of sites.entries()) {
  /* ... */
}
```

Do not introduce Babel-only or experimental proposals unless the project already depends on them.
